<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Essential 3.2 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-probability-iii" component="essential" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Probability III</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Will it rain on sports day? Will the next matatu have an empty seat? You deal with chance every day, and you have already used probability to measure how likely an event is on a scale from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will calculate theoretical probabilities from equally likely outcomes and work with discrete probabilities in different situations. You will apply the laws of probability, find the probability of combined events that are mutually exclusive or independent, and use tree diagrams to organise problems that happen in more than one stage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Probability is used in weather forecasting, in setting insurance premiums, in testing the quality of goods and in medicine, wherever a decision has to be made without certainty.
    </p>
  </introduction>

